thousands of former prisoners who  thought they would spend Christmas  behind bars are free thanks to the help  of the first step act President Trump  signed the legislation into law last  December the act increases the number of  good conduct time credits an inmate can  earn per year it also built on the Fair  Sentencing Act of 2010 signed by former  President Obama which reduced penalties  for crack cocaine offenses the US  Sentencing Commission says those changes  can help more than 140,000 people in  prison we spoke with two former inmates  who say they're still adjusting to their  newfound freedom before President Trump  signed the first step' Act into law did  you expect to spend the rest of your  life behind bars I did but I didn't  because I never gave up fighting 48  year-old Brian Johnson says it feels  good to be back in his old neighborhood  in Baltimore nearly 20 years ago Johnson  was convicted of money laundering and  drug distribution he was sentenced to  life in federal prison but when  President Trump signed off on the  first-step Act last December it meant  Johnson and thousands of others were  eligible for an earlier release or a  reduced sentence on July 9th Johnson  took his first step out of prison who  did you first lay eyes on when you got  out of prison the first person that I  sang was my oldest daughter how was she  31 you've missed most of her upbringing  20 years someone she pulled out it was  it was like an unbelievable feeling into  alcoho  he says he makes about $500 per week  working full-time for a delivery company  from prison life to this life I think  the assumption is oh you're free you  must be so excited it must be so great  it's great but will come with the  greatness is reality now you're back out  here cuz I mean you're gonna cost  already basically everything is free  food is free sleeping this fee in the  free world whether they say you've got  to pay for everything has that been the  biggest challenge for you actually no  technology has been amazing  for me right now I have a phone but I  couldn't tell you how to work everything  on the phone even harder than that  finding out he has a son since I've no  home I found out I have a son that's 26  years old and he is incarcerated in a  federal system for what 12 years for  carjacking does that hurt  yes they do because I feel like if I was  here that definitely man that recently  Johnson traveled to Washington DC with  the criminal justice reform group cut 50  which aims to reduce the prison  population by half he shared his story  with lawmakers fighting on behalf of  others who are still locked up there's a  lot we have left to do Holly Harris a  conservative and executive director of  the Justice Action Network pushed to  past the first step act if you had told  me 20 years ago that I would be working  with the ACLU and the n-double-a-cp I  would have called you crazy according to  the prison policy initiative the United  States has the highest incarceration  rate and the largest prison population  worldwide with about 2.3 million inmates  this is truly an American issue when  you're in prison you're locked in but  when you return to society you're locked  out you're locked out of a productive  life  clover Perez of Long Island New York  says that's exactly what she feared  after being released from federal prison  almost a year early  she served eight years for immigration  fraud my biggest concern was finding a  job I thought that I may not be  marketable and how was I going to catch  up she eventually found a job as an  office manager at a law firm her biggest  struggle she says coming to grips with  her oldest son's death he was shot and  killed about a year before she was  arrested when I come home I'll wait in  for Orangery to show up and he doesn't  show up it's a battle that I'm fighting  and I fight it every day and every day  as more inmates work to take their first  step out of  system the next step is making sure they  don't go back are you  a danger to society should people be  concerned about you people should not be  concerned about me I don't have nothing  to hide from the bottle intimidate no by  I go to work and I go home I know for a  fact that I'm a totally different person  this Danny's Johnson says prosecutors  are actually trying to appeal his  release and he's not alone a Reuters  investigation found dozens of cases  where the Justice Department tried to  keep offenders in prison most of those  attempts were unsuccessful so you know  the takeaway here really is it's not an  easy transition no I mean the first step  is obviously getting your freedom but  the next step is as we say at the end of  the piece really staying out of that  system right and you need the support in  order to do that when you realize as you  said that a hundred and forty thousand  people could be potentially affected by  this legislation that President Trump  has signed it will be and enduring  hallmark of president Trump's legacy so  absolutely and when you think about the  bipartisan right support they support  that exactly I mean that's why it was so  huge because even Holly Harris who you  heard in that piece she said that this  was something people told her this is  gonna take ten or fifteen years to pass  and here we are today and criminal  justice reform whether you're Republican  or Democrat people are coming together  and saying this is an issue we need to  do more about it first step you've got  to worry about that next step and your  jobs and skills need to be worked on  once they're not absolutely really  important reporting tarika thank you  very much for that  you
 

